### F2. Ports and the action model

**Depends on:** F1.

**Files:** `src/Domain/src/Dispute/Port/`, `src/Domain/src/Dispute/Port/Request/`

**What to do:**

Ports in the house shape — Request in, Outcome out:

```php
final readonly class DisputeAction
{
    public function __construct(
        public ActionType $type,               // RESPOND | ACCEPT | ACCEPT_PARTIAL
        public Execution $execution,           // API | OPERATOR
        public ?string $externalUrl,           // deep link into the portal, OPERATOR only
        public ?EvidenceRequirements $requirements,
        public \DateTimeImmutable $respondBy,
    ) {}
}

interface DisputeActionsPort
{
    public function availableActions(AvailableActionsRequest $request): DisputeActionSet;
}

interface SubmitEvidencePort
{
    public function submit(SubmitEvidenceRequest $request): SubmissionOutcome;
}

interface AcceptDisputePort
{
    /** Partial amount, when the provider supports it, rides on the request. */
    public function accept(AcceptDisputeRequest $request): AcceptOutcome;
}
```

Three interfaces rather than one, because no provider implements all three the same way: ConnexPay
has no API submission at all and would be forced to stub two methods it can never honour. This is
the same reason `PaymentIntent` has `CapturePort`, `CancelPort` and `RefundPort` separately.

`AcceptOutcome` is not `void`: it carries what the provider did (accepted in full / accepted
partially / already closed) so the aggregate records a fact rather than an assumption.

**Critical requirement:** `availableActions` must **not** return an empty set merely because the
provider offers no API action. An empty set means "this dispute isn't waiting on us": won, lost,
accepted, expired, or challenging is disallowed by the rules.

When an action is possible but a human performs it, return `Execution::OPERATOR` plus an
`externalUrl`. This applies to all of ConnexPay.

**Also here:** the cross-gateway recorder contract, in the Gateway package, next to its siblings:

```
src/Gateway/src/Webhook/Recorder/GatewayDisputeRecorder.php
```

```php
interface GatewayDisputeRecorder
{
    public function onDisputeObserved(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $paymentIntentId, DisputeSnapshot $snapshot): RecorderOutcome;

    public function onDisputeResolved(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $disputeRef, DisputeResolution $resolution): RecorderOutcome;

    /** ConnexPay only: a case we could not attach to any PaymentIntent. */
    public function onUnmatchedDispute(GatewayId $gatewayId, UnmatchedDispute $case): RecorderOutcome;
}
```

Like the other recorders it takes internal ids as plain strings — the Gateway package stays free of
domain VOs (see the `TransactionIdResolver` docblock). The implementation is the application's.

**Done when:** interfaces exist plus an in-memory fake for tests, and a test asserts that a fake
ConnexPay-like adapter returns a non-empty set with `Execution::OPERATOR`.

